  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nA few specks of flurries that passed through some isolated west to \nnorthwest portions of the area this morning have since passed off to \nthe east or dissipated. A few flurry chances (10-15% chance mainly \neast of HWY-281) will be possible to return tonight into Wednesday \nmorning as a low from southern Alberta/Saskatchewan dips down into \nthe central Plains, merging with ongoing Central Plains troughing. \nAccumulating snow seems highly unlikely at this time as most models \n(aside from the ECMWF registering a narrow bands of a trace of \nsnow) keep snow probs mainly far into northeastern Nebraska and \nwestern Iowa (areas best favored for accumulating snow). \n\nBesides the flurry chances tonight/Wednesday morning, temperatures \nthrough Thursday will continue to peak each day in the upper 30s and \n40s with overnight lows in the upper teens to mid 20s. Following the \nexit of a surface high pressure center today, steady southerly winds \n(5-15 mph, gusting as 20 mph this afternoon) will gradually lighten \ntonight. Winds for Wednesday and Thursday should remain fairly light \n(5-10 mph) with directions, though occasionally more variable, \ngenerally out of the south. The only last forecast element that could \ncome into play tonight for a few far northwest portions of the area \nis fog (10-30% chance). The latest HRRR/RRFS guidance continues to \nkeep any potential pockets of dense fog outside (west) of the area. \n\n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nThe next pattern shift will come over the weekend as the upper-level \njet aims northwest flow across the Central Plains region. Before \nthen, highs are currently forecast to lift into the upper 40s to mid \n50s Friday and Saturday. Don't expect for this mini-warmup to be \nlong lasting as the next chance for a cold frontal system (80-90% \nconfidence) now looks to fall Saturday afternoon/evening (could even \npotentially impact highs for Saturday?).  \n\nBased on consistent ECMWF/GFS guidance, an embedded shortwave trough \nremains possible to slip through the area Saturday, brining the area \nits next precipitation chance (25-40% chances) and placing a cap on \nthe mini end-of-week warmup. As far as precipitation chances go, the \ncurrent forecast keeps all precipitation Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning as rain (lows in the 30s). Given the near-freezing overnight \ntemperatures, the chance for a rain/snow mix will have to be \nmonitored closely depending on how the temperature forecast trends \nbehind the front (steady cold air advection pattern). The latest NBM \nguidance gives the full area at least a 40% chance of seeing \ntemperatures reach freezing at some point in the night with up to 60-\n80% confidence across much of south central Nebraska. The ultimate \nrain/snow probabilities will become highly dependent on how the \nprecipitation/temperature timing trends across the next few days. \n\nThe most aggressive model for snow as of now (ECMWF) shows a \nwidespread trace to just over 1\" of snow falling across the majority \nof area overnight. As of now, no other model guidance has been as \naggressive with the snow potential thus far. It is important to note \nthat this event is still 4 days out, so precipitation timing and \ntemperature trends may still have the potential to wiggle some as \nthis event comes into range of more higher-resolution, short-term \nmodel guidance. Beyond the precipitation chance over the weekend, \ntemperatures to start next week will look to favor the mid 40s to \nlow 50s.",
